\chapter*{Conventions}
\addcontentsline{toc}{chapter}{Conventions}
The metric, Fourier phases and continuum operator normalization are those of Parts I--II,
\[
\eta_{\mu\nu}=\operatorname{diag}(+1,-1,-1,-1),\qquad
\ee^{-\ii p\cdot x}=\ee^{-\ii p^0t+\ii\mathbf p\cdot\mathbf x},
\]
\[
[a(\mathbf p),a^\dagger(\mathbf q)]=(2\pi)^3\delta^{(3)}(\mathbf p-\mathbf q),
\qquad
|\mathbf p\rangle_{\rm inv}=\sqrt{2E_{\mathbf p}}\,a^\dagger(\mathbf p)|0\rangle.
\]
For electromagnetism,
\[
A^\mu=(A^0,\Afield),\qquad
A_\mu\longrightarrow A_\mu+\partial_\mu\alpha,
\]
so in three-vector notation
\[
A^0\longrightarrow A^0+\partial_t\alpha,
\qquad
\Afield\longrightarrow\Afield-\nabla\alpha.
\]
The electromagnetic convention is inherited rather than chosen anew in this Part. The transformation law of charged matter will not be assumed here; it will be fixed only when the Maxwell gauge orbit is coupled to the already-quantized complex scalar. Every subsequent sign is then derived from those inherited conventions and that coupling requirement rather than imported from a different gauge orientation.

