\mainmatter

\chapter{When the charged source becomes dynamical}

Part II coupled Maxwell theory to a \emph{prescribed} conserved source. That construction used only
\[
\partial_\mu J^\mu=0,
\]
so a boundary-trivial change \(A_\mu\to A_\mu+\partial_\mu\alpha\) changed the source action by a boundary term. A charged scalar is not prescribed independently of the electromagnetic field: once the coupling is switched on, the interaction also changes the scalar equation of motion and with it the current that can consistently source Maxwell's equation.

The charge sectors themselves are already fixed. For the free complex scalar,
\begin{equation}
:Q:=\int\frac{\dd^3p}{(2\pi)^3}
\left[a^\dagger(\mathbf p)a(\mathbf p)-b^\dagger(\mathbf p)b(\mathbf p)\right],
\label{eq:p3-free-Q}
\end{equation}
and a species with particle charge \(q\) has \(Q_{\rm phys}=qQ\). Thus \(a^\dagger|0\rangle\) and \(b^\dagger|0\rangle\) carry \(+q\) and \(-q\). The corresponding free charge current is
\begin{equation}
\boxed{
J_{(0)}^\mu
=\ii q\left[
\Phi^*\partial^\mu\Phi-(\partial^\mu\Phi^*)\Phi
\right].}
\label{eq:p3-free-charge-current}
\end{equation}
For a positive-frequency free plane wave \(\Phi=A\ee^{-\ii p\cdot x}\), \(p^0>0\),
\begin{equation}
J_{(0)}^\mu=2q p^\mu|A|^2,
\label{eq:p3-plane-wave-current}
\end{equation}
so the sign of \(J^0\) follows the signed charge on the particle branch.

\section{The free current fails as a dynamical Maxwell source}

The most economical first attempt is to reuse the free current in the source term,
\begin{equation}
\Lagr_{\rm try}
=\partial_\mu\Phi^*\partial^\mu\Phi-m^2|\Phi|^2
-\frac14F_{\mu\nu}F^{\mu\nu}
-A_\mu J_{(0)}^\mu.
\label{eq:p3-linear-try}
\end{equation}
Varying \(A_\nu\) gives
\begin{equation}
\partial_\mu F^{\mu\nu}=J_{(0)}^\nu.
\label{eq:p3-linear-try-Maxwell}
\end{equation}
The antisymmetry of \(F^{\mu\nu}\) makes the left-hand side identically divergence-free:
\begin{equation}
\partial_\nu\partial_\mu F^{\mu\nu}=0.
\label{eq:p3-Maxwell-div-identity}
\end{equation}
A dynamical source appearing on the right of Maxwell's equation must therefore satisfy \(\partial_\mu J^\mu=0\) on the coupled equations.

That requirement is not met by the free current. Varying \(\Phi^*\) in \cref{eq:p3-linear-try} gives
\begin{align}
0={}&\partial_\mu\!\left(\partial^\mu\Phi+\ii qA^\mu\Phi\right)
+m^2\Phi+\ii qA_\mu\partial^\mu\Phi\\
={}&(\Box+m^2)\Phi
+\ii q(\partial_\mu A^\mu)\Phi
+2\ii qA^\mu\partial_\mu\Phi,
\label{eq:p3-linear-try-matter-eom}
\end{align}
while the conjugate equation is
\begin{equation}
(\Box+m^2)\Phi^*
-\ii q(\partial_\mu A^\mu)\Phi^*
-2\ii qA^\mu\partial_\mu\Phi^*=0.
\label{eq:p3-linear-try-matter-eom-conj}
\end{equation}
The divergence of the free current is
\begin{align}
\partial_\mu J_{(0)}^\mu
&=\ii q\left(\Phi^*\Box\Phi-\Phi\Box\Phi^*\right)\\
&=2q^2\left[(\partial_\mu A^\mu)|\Phi|^2
+A^\mu\partial_\mu|\Phi|^2\right]\\
&=\boxed{2q^2\partial_\mu\!\left(A^\mu|\Phi|^2\right)}.
\label{eq:p3-free-current-not-conserved}
\end{align}
The equation \cref{eq:p3-linear-try-Maxwell} demands zero divergence, whereas the matter equation generated by the trial Lagrangian gives \cref{eq:p3-free-current-not-conserved}. The failure is more specific than a loss of global charge conservation. The trial matter Lagrangian is still invariant under a \emph{constant} phase rotation, but its Noether current is no longer the free current because the derivative coupling has changed the canonical momenta. Applying the Noether construction to \cref{eq:p3-linear-try} gives
\begin{equation}
J_{\rm try}^\mu
=J_{(0)}^\mu-2q^2A^\mu|\Phi|^2,
\qquad
\partial_\mu J_{\rm try}^\mu=0
\label{eq:p3-interacting-current-preview}
\end{equation}
on the trial matter equation. Equation \eqref{eq:p3-free-current-not-conserved} is exactly the cancellation needed for this conservation law.

The inconsistency is therefore an interface failure: Maxwell's equation in \cref{eq:p3-linear-try-Maxwell} was sourced by \(J_{(0)}^\mu\), while the coupled matter theory conserves \(J_{\rm try}^\mu\). A consistent local theory must make the dynamical Maxwell source and the interacting charge current coincide. The question is what local principle produces that correction in the field equation rather than inserting it by hand.

\section{Local rephasing and the ordinary derivative}

Maxwell theory has already supplied an equivalence relation among potentials,
\[
A_\mu' = A_\mu+\partial_\mu\alpha.
\]
The free scalar also has a genuine global charge symmetry \(\Phi\to e^{-\ii\beta}\Phi\), generated by the dimensionless charge \(Q\) of Part II. Equivalently, writing \(\beta=q\alpha_0\), \(U(\alpha_0)=e^{+\ii\alpha_0Q_{\rm phys}}\) with \(Q_{\rm phys}=qQ\). This global transformation and a Maxwell gauge-representative change have different status.
\newpage
To repair the source mismatch while preserving the physical equivalence of boundary-trivial Maxwell representatives, seek a local action on the charged field that uses the already established charge representation. The minimal local choice is
\begin{equation}
\Phi'(x)=\ee^{-\ii q\alpha(x)}\Phi(x).
\label{eq:p3-local-phase}
\end{equation}
For gauge functions that are trivial at the boundary, this local rephasing relates descriptions on one gauge orbit and carries no additional observable charge. Constant global phase rotations generated by \(Q_{\rm phys}\), and more generally boundary-nontrivial gauge transformations, are not being quotiented away by that statement. The local phase law is therefore a construction of the gauge-representative action on charged matter, not a new independent global symmetry postulate.

The obstruction sits in the ordinary derivative:
\begin{align}
\partial_\mu\Phi'
&=\partial_\mu\!\left(\ee^{-\ii q\alpha}\Phi\right)\\
&=\ee^{-\ii q\alpha}
\left[\partial_\mu\Phi-\ii q(\partial_\mu\alpha)\Phi\right].
\label{eq:p3-ordinary-derivative-failure}
\end{align}
Nearby phases are being compared as though their local conventions had already been identified.

For
\begin{equation}
\Lagr_0=\partial_\mu\Phi^*\partial^\mu\Phi-m^2|\Phi|^2,
\label{eq:p3-free-complex-L}
\end{equation}
the first-order local variation is
\begin{align}
\delta\Lagr_0
&=\ii q(\partial_\mu\alpha)
\left[\Phi^*\partial^\mu\Phi-(\partial^\mu\Phi^*)\Phi\right]\\
&=(\partial_\mu\alpha)J_{(0)}^\mu.
\label{eq:p3-local-L-variation}
\end{align}
The sign in this equation is fixed by the already chosen convention \(\Phi'=e^{-\ii q\alpha}\Phi\); no independent gauge-orientation choice remains.

